      "section": "What I’m experimenting with —",
      "category": "other",
      "title": "Grayscaling my phone",
      "creator": null,
      "note": "This was recommended to me by comedian Whitney Cummings, and it has been\nstudied for dramatically reducing smart phone and social media use.Here are\n[instructions from\nLifehacker](https://el2.fourhourmail.com/c/68u8wxgppu8h5m07g/zvtghn/aHR0cHM6Ly9saWZlaGFja2VyLmNvbS9jaGFuZ2UteW91ci1zY3JlZW4tdG8tZ3JheXNjYWxlLXRvLWNvbWJhdC1waG9uZS1hZGRpY3RpLTE3OTU4MjE4NDM=):\n\"The process for enabling grayscale differs for different models of Android\nphones, but it’s typically accessed via the “Accessibility” menu. In iOS 10,\ngo to Settings > General > Accessibility >Display Accommodations >Color\nFilters. Switch Color Filters on and select Grayscale. To easily toggle\nbetween color and grayscale, go to Settings > General > Accessibility >\nAccessibility Shortcut > Color Filters. Now, you just press the home button\nthree times to enable grayscale. Triple-click again to go back to\ncolor.\"**",
      "original_url": "https://el2.fourhourmail.com/c/68u8wxgppu8h5m07g/opfkhq/aHR0cHM6Ly9saWZlaGFja2VyLmNvbS9jaGFuZ2UteW91ci1zY3JlZW4tdG8tZ3JheXNjYWxlLXRvLWNvbWJhdC1waG9uZS1hZGRpY3RpLTE3OTU4MjE4NDM=",
